# Week 01 Journal

| Field | Notes |
|---|---|
| **Date** | October 8, 2026 |
| **What I learned that was genuinely new** | I learned that an LLM generates an answer one token at a time by predicting what is likely to come next. This helped me understand why a fluent and confident answer is not necessarily a correct one. I also learned that some features that look like AI can actually be implemented using simple rules and logic. |
| **One AI output I initially trusted** | I initially trusted the source links and citations provided by AI tools for Q1, Q2, Q3, Q8, Q9 and Q10. I also initially trusted Gemini's list of "Cummings guidelines" for Q4. |
| **How I verified it** | I searched for each source and opened the links myself. I found that some links were wrong or outdated, one paper mentioned for Q9 could not be found, and the NEC source given for Q10 did not match the resistance values I was using. For Q4, I compared Gemini's guidelines with the actual Cummings paper and found that one of the listed guidelines was not present in the paper. For Q5, I checked the HTTP status-code claims against RFC 9110. I also found that some screenshot links were returning 404 errors because of a double `.png` extension and fixed the links. |
| **What the AI did well** | The AI explained the concepts clearly, helped me organize my answers, and gave me useful examples and structures for the assignment. It was especially useful for getting a starting point and identifying areas that I needed to investigate further. |
| **What the AI could not be trusted to decide** | I learned that I should not rely on AI alone to decide whether a source or link actually exists, whether an exact number is supported by a reliable source, or whether a named paper actually contains a particular statement. |
| **One question I still have** | How are chat models trained after the basic next-token prediction stage so that they can follow instructions? Also, how do they decide whether to choose the most likely token or sample another possible token? |
| **What I will do differently next week** | I will open and check every important link before using it, record verification results while I work instead of waiting until the end, and check the publication date and reliability of each source. I will also avoid accepting precise claims just because they are presented confidently or with a citation. |
